"""
knowledge_base.py
------------------
The AZ-900 "knowledge base" — every fact and practice question the app is
grounded in. Still a plain, hand-editable Python file on purpose (not a
database or vector store):

  - Easy for a non-engineer teammate to open this file and add/edit a fact
    or question without touching any other code.
  - Removes the #1 risk for the highest-stakes content in the app (the
    questions used to score a learner's mastery): every question below
    traces back to a real, cited source, not a fresh LLM call per question.

WHERE THIS CONTENT CAME FROM: this is no longer a small hand-typed starter
set. It was built by (1) pulling Microsoft's official AZ-900 "Skills
measured" outline (learn.microsoft.com/en-us/credentials/certifications/
resources/study-guides/az-900) — all 57 official skill bullets across the 3
domains, plus their real exam weights — then (2) fetching the actual
Microsoft Learn training-module unit page backing each bullet and writing
ORIGINAL facts/questions grounded in that real content (never copied
verbatim, never a real/leaked exam question), and (3) adding 5 more topics
(sustainability, AI/ML/IoT-Edge, encryption/key management, cost
optimization, the Service Trust Portal) that Microsoft's own training
covers but that aren't part of the official 57-bullet outline. The full
per-concept source citations (which Learn page every fact came from) live
in backend/app/learning/content_bank/*.json — this file is the
merged/flattened result actually used by the app; that folder is the
audit trail if a fact ever needs to be re-verified or a citation checked.

HOW THIS FILE IS USED ELSEWHERE (so you know what breaks if you rename things):
  - `learning/service.py` imports DOMAINS, DOMAIN_WEIGHTS, QUESTION_BANK,
    SNIPPETS, and get_snippet_for_topic(). It samples QUESTION_BANK for the
    diagnostic quiz (spread across domains using DOMAIN_WEIGHTS, not evenly
    — see start_assessment), hands SNIPPETS to the LLM as "facts you must
    base your questions on" when generating a personalized practice game,
    and averages domain mastery using DOMAIN_WEIGHTS (the real 25-30/
    35-40/30-35 exam weighting) instead of a flat 1/3 each — see
    get_progress_summary.
  - Every dict in QUESTION_BANK has a "topic" key. Many questions share the
    same topic (all the practice questions written for one skill bullet),
    which is fine — sampling doesn't care about topic granularity. Each
    question also carries its own "explanation" now (submit_assessment
    prefers that over the older topic-level snippet lookup, falling back
    to get_snippet_for_topic only if a question has none).

HOW TO ADD MORE CONTENT:
  1. Pick one of the 3 DOMAINS below (these must match the real AZ-900 exam
     objective domains — don't add a 4th without also updating the frontend,
     which assumes exactly 3 domains for its progress bars).
  2. Add a new dict to that domain's SNIPPETS list with a short, unique
     "topic" slug (lowercase_with_underscores) and a 1-3 sentence "snippet"
     that is a genuinely accurate Azure fact — cite where you got it from in
     a nearby content_bank/*.json file if it came from a specific source.
  3. Add one or more matching dicts to that domain's QUESTION_BANK list,
     reusing the SAME "topic" slug, with a "question", exactly 4 "choices",
     the 0-indexed "answerIndex" of the correct choice, and an "explanation"
     of why that answer is right.
"""

import random

# The 3 official AZ-900 exam objective domains. The frontend dashboard and
# progress bar assume there are exactly 3 of these — if you add or remove a
# domain here, you'll also need to touch the AZ-900 React components.
DOMAINS = [
    "Cloud Concepts",
    "Azure Architecture & Services",
    "Azure Management & Governance",
]

# The REAL AZ-900 exam weighting (percentage midpoints of Microsoft's
# published 25-30% / 35-40% / 30-35% ranges) — used by service.py to weight
# both the diagnostic's question spread and the overall mastery average, so
# "Program Progress" reflects the actual exam's emphasis instead of
# pretending all 3 domains count equally.
DOMAIN_WEIGHTS = {
    "Cloud Concepts": 27.5,
    "Azure Architecture & Services": 37.5,
    "Azure Management & Governance": 32.5,
}

