        {"topic": "directory_services_entra_id_and_domain_services", "question": "What is Microsoft Entra ID?", "choices": ["A cloud-based identity and access management service used to authenticate and authorize users to applications and resources", "A physical networking appliance", "A database engine for storing files", "A virtual machine sizing tool"], "answerIndex": 0, "explanation": "Microsoft Entra ID is Microsoft's cloud identity platform for authenticating and authorizing access to resources and apps."},
        {"topic": "directory_services_entra_id_and_domain_services", "question": "A company wants to lift-and-shift a legacy application that requires classic domain join and group policy, without deploying its own domain controllers in Azure. What should they use?", "choices": ["Microsoft Entra Domain Services", "Microsoft Entra ID alone", "Azure Migrate", "Azure Data Box"], "answerIndex": 0, "explanation": "Entra Domain Services offers managed domain-join, LDAP, and Kerberos/NTLM support without requiring self-managed domain controllers."},
        {"topic": "directory_services_entra_id_and_domain_services", "question": "What is a key difference between Microsoft Entra ID and Microsoft Entra Domain Services?", "choices": ["Entra ID is a cloud identity platform for modern authentication protocols, while Entra Domain Services provides traditional domain-join and LDAP/Kerberos capabilities as a managed service", "They are identical products with different names", "Entra Domain Services only works on-premises", "Entra ID requires deploying physical domain controllers"], "answerIndex": 0, "explanation": "Entra ID targets modern cloud authentication, while Entra Domain Services delivers classic domain services as a managed offering."},
        {"topic": "directory_services_entra_id_and_domain_services", "question": "Which statement about Microsoft Entra Domain Services is correct?", "choices": ["It removes the need to deploy, manage, and patch your own domain controllers while still supporting protocols like LDAP and Kerberos", "It is identical to on-premises Active Directory with no differences", "It cannot be used alongside Microsoft Entra ID", "It only supports cloud-native OAuth authentication"], "answerIndex": 0, "explanation": "Entra Domain Services is a managed service that provides traditional protocol support without requiring self-managed domain controllers."},
        {"topic": "directory_services_entra_id_and_domain_services", "question": "An organization wants a single place to manage user accounts that can sign into Microsoft 365, Azure resources, and third-party SaaS applications. What should they use?", "choices": ["Microsoft Entra ID", "Microsoft Entra Domain Services", "Azure Key Vault", "Microsoft Defender for Cloud"], "answerIndex": 0, "explanation": "Microsoft Entra ID centrally manages identities used to sign into Microsoft 365, Azure, and connected SaaS applications."},
        {"topic": "directory_services_entra_id_and_domain_services", "question": "Which capability is characteristic of Microsoft Entra ID rather than traditional on-premises Active Directory?", "choices": ["Native support for modern authentication standards like SAML, OAuth, and OpenID Connect for cloud application sign-in", "Support for classic Group Policy Objects", "Requiring on-premises domain controllers", "Using only Kerberos for all authentication"], "answerIndex": 0, "explanation": "Microsoft Entra ID is built around modern, cloud-native authentication protocols rather than classic on-premises mechanisms."},
        {"topic": "directory_services_entra_id_and_domain_services", "question": "How do Entra ID and Entra Domain Services typically work together in a hybrid scenario?", "choices": ["Entra ID can synchronize identities from on-premises AD DS, while Entra Domain Services can provide a separate managed domain used by legacy apps migrated to Azure", "They cannot coexist in the same tenant", "Entra Domain Services replaces Entra ID entirely", "Entra ID requires Entra Domain Services to function at all"], "answerIndex": 0, "explanation": "The two services can complement each other: Entra ID handles modern identity sync, while Entra Domain Services supports legacy domain needs."},
        {"topic": "authentication_methods_sso_mfa_passwordless", "question": "What is the main benefit of single sign-on (SSO)?", "choices": ["Users authenticate once and can then access multiple applications without repeated sign-in prompts", "It eliminates the need for any authentication", "It automatically encrypts all stored data", "It replaces the need for role-based access control"], "answerIndex": 0, "explanation": "SSO lets a single authentication event grant access across multiple apps, reducing repeated sign-in prompts."},
        {"topic": "authentication_methods_sso_mfa_passwordless", "question": "A security team wants to reduce the risk of account compromise from a single stolen password without requiring users to memorize a second password. What should they implement?", "choices": ["Multifactor authentication", "Single sign-on only", "A longer password policy alone", "Disabling authentication logging"], "answerIndex": 0, "explanation": "MFA adds a second, non-password verification factor (like a device or biometric), reducing risk without requiring a second memorized password."},
        {"topic": "authentication_methods_sso_mfa_passwordless", "question": "How does passwordless authentication differ from traditional multifactor authentication that still uses a password?", "choices": ["Passwordless authentication eliminates the password factor entirely, relying instead on a possessed device plus a PIN or biometric, whereas traditional MFA still uses a password as one factor", "Passwordless authentication is another name for single sign-on", "Traditional MFA never uses a password", "Passwordless authentication requires three separate passwords"], "answerIndex": 0, "explanation": "Passwordless methods drop the password entirely, unlike traditional MFA, which typically still includes a password as one factor."},
        {"topic": "authentication_methods_sso_mfa_passwordless", "question": "Which statement about MFA is correct?", "choices": ["MFA combines two or more categories of verification, such as a password plus a one-time code from an app, to confirm a user's identity", "MFA only requires a username and password", "MFA is only available for on-premises Active Directory", "MFA replaces the need for a directory service entirely"], "answerIndex": 0, "explanation": "MFA requires at least two independent verification factors, strengthening identity confirmation beyond a password alone."},
        {"topic": "authentication_methods_sso_mfa_passwordless", "question": "An organization wants employees to sign in using a FIDO2 security key and a PIN instead of typing a password. Which authentication approach does this describe?", "choices": ["Passwordless authentication", "Single sign-on", "Role-based access control", "Conditional Access without any verification"], "answerIndex": 0, "explanation": "Signing in with a FIDO2 key and PIN, with no password involved, is a classic example of passwordless authentication."},
        {"topic": "authentication_methods_sso_mfa_passwordless", "question": "Which of the following is an example of a passwordless sign-in method?", "choices": ["Microsoft Authenticator app push notification combined with a PIN or biometric on the registered device", "Typing a username and password only", "Answering a security question only", "Entering a shared static password"], "answerIndex": 0, "explanation": "A device-bound Authenticator prompt combined with a PIN or biometric verifies identity without ever using a password."},
        {"topic": "authentication_methods_sso_mfa_passwordless", "question": "What problem does SSO primarily solve compared to MFA?", "choices": ["SSO reduces how often a user must sign in across multiple apps, while MFA strengthens the security of each sign-in by requiring extra verification", "SSO and MFA both eliminate the need for passwords entirely", "SSO increases the number of passwords a user must remember", "MFA reduces the number of times a user signs in"], "answerIndex": 0, "explanation": "SSO addresses sign-in convenience across apps, while MFA addresses the strength and security of each individual sign-in."},
        {"topic": "external_identities", "question": "What is the purpose of external identities in Microsoft Entra ID?", "choices": ["To let organizations collaborate with and authenticate users outside their own organization, such as partners or customers, without creating full internal accounts for them", "To disable all guest access to a tenant", "To provide physical security for datacenters", "To replace role-based access control"], "answerIndex": 0, "explanation": "External identities enable secure collaboration with outside users without the overhead of full internal account creation."},
        {"topic": "external_identities", "question": "A company wants to invite a vendor's employee to collaborate on a shared project using the vendor's own company credentials. Which capability supports this?", "choices": ["B2B collaboration", "B2C customer identity", "Conditional Access alone", "Azure Migrate"], "answerIndex": 0, "explanation": "B2B collaboration lets external partners sign in as guests using their own organizational credentials."},
        {"topic": "external_identities", "question": "How does B2B collaboration differ from B2C in Microsoft Entra External ID?", "choices": ["B2B invites external partner or vendor users as guests into your organization's directory, while B2C manages authentication for external customers of a consumer-facing application", "B2B and B2C are the exact same feature", "B2C is used only for internal employees", "B2B is used only for public consumer apps"], "answerIndex": 0, "explanation": "B2B is for inviting guest collaborators into your directory, while B2C serves customer identity needs for external-facing apps."},
        {"topic": "external_identities", "question": "Which statement about external identities is correct?", "choices": ["A guest user invited via B2B collaboration can sign in using credentials from their own home organization rather than needing a new account created by the inviting tenant", "External identities require every guest to have a brand-new account created and managed by the host tenant", "External identities cannot be governed by access policies", "B2B collaboration only works for employees within the same tenant"], "answerIndex": 0, "explanation": "Guest users authenticate with their existing home-organization or personal credentials, avoiding duplicate account management."},
        {"topic": "external_identities", "question": "A retail company is building a mobile app for public customers to create their own accounts and sign in. What should they use?", "choices": ["Azure AD B2C / Microsoft Entra External ID for customers", "B2B collaboration", "Microsoft Entra Domain Services", "Azure RBAC"], "answerIndex": 0, "explanation": "B2C-style customer identity capabilities are designed for consumer-facing apps where the public creates and manages their own accounts."},
        {"topic": "external_identities", "question": "Which of these is a benefit of using external identities instead of creating standard internal user accounts for outside users?", "choices": ["External users can authenticate with credentials they already have, reducing administrative overhead of managing extra passwords", "It removes all security controls for those users", "It grants automatic Global Administrator access", "It disables auditing for external sign-ins"], "answerIndex": 0, "explanation": "Allowing external users to reuse their existing credentials reduces the burden of creating and managing separate accounts for them."},
        {"topic": "external_identities", "question": "Which scenario best illustrates a B2B guest access use case rather than a B2C use case?", "choices": ["Granting a consultant from a partner firm temporary access to an internal SharePoint site using their own organizational login", "A retail customer creating a new account to shop on a public e-commerce website", "An anonymous visitor browsing a public marketing page", "An internal employee logging into payroll software"], "answerIndex": 0, "explanation": "Inviting a partner's employee as a guest to access internal resources is the classic B2B collaboration scenario."},
        {"topic": "conditional_access", "question": "What is Microsoft Entra Conditional Access?", "choices": ["A policy engine that grants or restricts access based on signals like user, location, device, and application at sign-in time", "A backup service for user accounts", "A tool for provisioning virtual machines", "A billing and cost management dashboard"], "answerIndex": 0, "explanation": "Conditional Access evaluates contextual signals at sign-in and applies automated decisions to grant, restrict, or block access."},
        {"topic": "conditional_access", "question": "A company wants to require MFA only when a user signs in from outside the corporate network, but not when signing in from a trusted office location. What feature enables this?", "choices": ["Conditional Access", "Azure RBAC", "Microsoft Entra Domain Services", "Azure Data Box"], "answerIndex": 0, "explanation": "Conditional Access policies can apply controls like MFA selectively, based on conditions such as network location."},
        {"topic": "conditional_access", "question": "How does Conditional Access differ from basic MFA enforcement alone?", "choices": ["Conditional Access applies context-aware if-then policies that can require MFA (or other controls) selectively based on signals, rather than always requiring the same static verification for every sign-in", "Conditional Access removes the need for authentication entirely", "MFA and Conditional Access are the exact same setting", "Conditional Access only controls network firewall rules"], "answerIndex": 0, "explanation": "Conditional Access is a policy engine that can invoke MFA conditionally, unlike a blanket always-on MFA requirement."},
        {"topic": "conditional_access", "question": "Which statement about Conditional Access policies is correct?", "choices": ["They can be configured to block access automatically when a sign-in is deemed risky or coming from a disallowed location", "They can only allow access and never block it", "They apply only to on-premises Active Directory, not Microsoft Entra ID", "They ignore device compliance status entirely"], "answerIndex": 0, "explanation": "Conditional Access policies can block access outright when risk signals or location conditions warrant it."},
        {"topic": "conditional_access", "question": "An admin wants to ensure only devices marked as compliant by Intune can access a sensitive finance application. Which tool would enforce this?", "choices": ["Conditional Access with a device compliance requirement", "Azure Data Box", "AzCopy", "Azure File Sync"], "answerIndex": 0, "explanation": "Conditional Access can require device compliance status as a condition for granting access to an application."},
        {"topic": "conditional_access", "question": "Which of these is a typical signal evaluated by a Conditional Access policy?", "choices": ["The sign-in location or network the request originates from", "The color scheme of the user's desktop", "The size of the user's mailbox", "The Azure region hosting a storage account"], "answerIndex": 0, "explanation": "Sign-in location and network are common signals Conditional Access evaluates to decide whether to grant or restrict access."},
        {"topic": "conditional_access", "question": "Conditional Access supports Zero Trust primarily by doing what?", "choices": ["Continuously evaluating access requests against real-time signals instead of granting broad, one-time trust after initial login", "Granting unrestricted access once a user is inside the corporate network", "Removing the need for identity verification", "Applying the same access rule to every user regardless of context"], "answerIndex": 0, "explanation": "Conditional Access continuously assesses context at each access attempt, aligning with Zero Trust's explicit-verification principle."},
        {"topic": "azure_rbac", "question": "What is the primary purpose of Azure RBAC?", "choices": ["To control what actions a user, group, or application can perform on specific Azure resources", "To encrypt data at rest", "To provide network connectivity between virtual networks", "To manage billing invoices"], "answerIndex": 0, "explanation": "RBAC governs authorization, determining what actions identities can take on which Azure resources."},
        {"topic": "azure_rbac", "question": "A manager should be able to view all resources in a subscription for reporting purposes but must not be able to modify anything. Which built-in role fits best?", "choices": ["Reader", "Owner", "Contributor", "User Access Administrator"], "answerIndex": 0, "explanation": "The Reader role grants view-only access without the ability to make changes to resources."},
        {"topic": "azure_rbac", "question": "What is the key difference between the Owner and Contributor built-in roles?", "choices": ["Owner can manage access for other users in addition to managing resources, while Contributor can manage resources but cannot grant access to others", "Contributor has more permissions than Owner", "Owner can only read resources", "Contributor and Owner are identical roles"], "answerIndex": 0, "explanation": "Owner includes the ability to assign roles to others, a permission that Contributor explicitly lacks."},
        {"topic": "azure_rbac", "question": "Which statement about RBAC scope is correct?", "choices": ["A role assigned at the subscription level is inherited by all resource groups and resources within that subscription unless overridden", "RBAC roles can only be assigned to individual resources, never to subscriptions", "Scope has no effect on where permissions apply", "Management groups cannot have RBAC roles assigned to them"], "answerIndex": 0, "explanation": "RBAC permissions granted at a higher scope, like a subscription, flow down and are inherited by resources beneath it."},
        {"topic": "azure_rbac", "question": "An organization wants a specific user to be able to assign roles to other users without giving them full control to modify resource configurations. Which built-in role is designed for this?", "choices": ["User Access Administrator", "Reader", "Contributor", "Owner"], "answerIndex": 0, "explanation": "User Access Administrator is scoped specifically to managing user access, without granting full resource management rights."},
        {"topic": "azure_rbac", "question": "Which three elements make up an RBAC role assignment?", "choices": ["Security principal, role definition, and scope", "Username, password, and MFA method", "Subscription, region, and resource group name only", "Storage account, container, and blob"], "answerIndex": 0, "explanation": "A role assignment binds a security principal to a role definition at a defined scope."},
        {"topic": "azure_rbac", "question": "How does RBAC differ from Conditional Access in what it controls?", "choices": ["RBAC controls what actions a user can perform on which resources, while Conditional Access controls the conditions under which a user is allowed to sign in at all", "RBAC and Conditional Access are the same feature", "Conditional Access assigns Owner and Contributor roles", "RBAC evaluates sign-in risk signals like device and location"], "answerIndex": 0, "explanation": "RBAC governs authorization to resources, while Conditional Access governs the sign-in conditions that grant or block authentication."},
        {"topic": "zero_trust", "question": "What is the core assumption behind the Zero Trust security model?", "choices": ["No request should be automatically trusted, even if it originates from inside the corporate network, so it must be verified explicitly", "Anyone inside the corporate firewall can be trusted by default", "Passwords alone are sufficient to secure all resources", "Only external users need to be verified"], "answerIndex": 0, "explanation": "Zero Trust rejects implicit trust based on network location, requiring explicit verification for every request."},
        {"topic": "zero_trust", "question": "A company redesigns its security approach so that every access request, whether from inside or outside the office, must be authenticated and authorized before granting access. Which model does this describe?", "choices": ["Zero Trust", "Defense in depth only", "Perimeter-only security", "Single sign-on only"], "answerIndex": 0, "explanation": "Requiring verification for every request regardless of network location is the defining characteristic of Zero Trust."},
        {"topic": "zero_trust", "question": "How does Zero Trust differ from traditional perimeter-based security?", "choices": ["Zero Trust verifies every request individually regardless of network location, while perimeter-based security largely trusts anything already inside the network boundary", "Zero Trust trusts all internal traffic automatically", "Perimeter security requires continuous verification of every request", "They are the same approach with different names"], "answerIndex": 0, "explanation": "Perimeter-based models grant broad trust once inside the network, while Zero Trust verifies continuously regardless of location."},
        {"topic": "zero_trust", "question": "Which of the following is one of the three guiding principles of Zero Trust?", "choices": ["Use least-privilege access", "Trust but verify only once per year", "Grant broad access by default to speed up productivity", "Disable multifactor authentication for internal users"], "answerIndex": 0, "explanation": "Least-privilege access is one of Zero Trust's three guiding principles, alongside verify explicitly and assume breach."},
        {"topic": "zero_trust", "question": "Under a Zero Trust approach, if a user's credentials are compromised, what design goal limits the damage an attacker can do?", "choices": ["Least-privilege access limits the resources the compromised account could reach", "Full administrative access is granted to every account so incident response is easier", "All users share one common account", "Network segmentation is intentionally avoided"], "answerIndex": 0, "explanation": "Limiting each identity's access to only what it needs reduces the blast radius if that identity is compromised."},
        {"topic": "zero_trust", "question": "What does \"assume breach\" mean in the context of Zero Trust?", "choices": ["Design and operate systems as though an attacker may already have a foothold, minimizing potential damage and blast radius", "Assume that no breach will ever occur and skip monitoring", "Assume every employee is malicious", "Assume breaches only happen to on-premises systems, not the cloud"], "answerIndex": 0, "explanation": "Assume breach means designing defenses and segmentation as if an attacker is already present, to limit potential damage."},
        {"topic": "zero_trust", "question": "Which statement best contrasts Zero Trust's \"verify explicitly\" principle with older security assumptions?", "choices": ["Verify explicitly means authentication and authorization decisions use all available signals every time, rather than assuming trust after one initial login", "Verify explicitly means passwords are no longer needed at all", "Older security models required more verification than Zero Trust does", "Verify explicitly only applies to external guest users"], "answerIndex": 0, "explanation": "Verify explicitly requires continuous evaluation of signals for each request, unlike older models that trusted a single login."},
        {"topic": "defense_in_depth", "question": "What is the primary idea behind the defense-in-depth security model?", "choices": ["Using multiple layered security controls so that if one layer fails, other layers still protect the system", "Relying entirely on a single strong firewall to secure everything", "Removing all security controls to simplify operations", "Using only physical security to protect cloud data"], "answerIndex": 0, "explanation": "Defense in depth layers multiple independent controls so a single failure doesn't fully compromise the system."},
        {"topic": "defense_in_depth", "question": "An organization implements physical datacenter security, network firewalls, identity verification, and data encryption all at once for a critical application. What strategy does this represent?", "choices": ["Defense in depth", "Zero Trust exclusively", "Single sign-on", "Role-based access control alone"], "answerIndex": 0, "explanation": "Combining several distinct protective layers across physical, network, identity, and data domains is the essence of defense in depth."},
        {"topic": "defense_in_depth", "question": "In the commonly referenced defense-in-depth layer model, which layer is typically placed at the center as the most protected asset?", "choices": ["Data", "Physical security", "Perimeter", "Network"], "answerIndex": 0, "explanation": "Data is treated as the most valuable asset and sits at the center, protected by all the surrounding layers."},
        {"topic": "defense_in_depth", "question": "Which statement about defense in depth is correct?", "choices": ["It relies on multiple independent layers of security controls rather than a single control to protect resources", "It assumes that if the outer perimeter is secure, no other layers are needed", "It is only relevant to on-premises datacenters, not the cloud", "It removes the need for identity and access management"], "answerIndex": 0, "explanation": "Defense in depth deliberately uses several independent layers rather than depending on any single control."},
        {"topic": "defense_in_depth", "question": "A network firewall at the perimeter layer fails to block an intrusion attempt. Under a defense-in-depth strategy, what should happen next?", "choices": ["Additional layers, such as identity controls, application security, and data encryption, still provide protection", "The entire environment becomes immediately and fully compromised with no further protection", "No further layers exist beyond the perimeter", "The attacker automatically gains access to all data"], "answerIndex": 0, "explanation": "Defense in depth ensures other layers continue to protect resources even if one layer, like the perimeter, is breached."},
        {"topic": "defense_in_depth", "question": "Which of the following is typically included as one of the outer layers in the defense-in-depth model, before reaching the network and compute layers?", "choices": ["Identity and access", "Database indexing", "Billing and cost management", "Application source code formatting"], "answerIndex": 0, "explanation": "Identity and access is one of the outer layers in the commonly referenced defense-in-depth model, ahead of network and compute."},
        {"topic": "defense_in_depth", "question": "How does defense in depth differ from relying solely on perimeter security?", "choices": ["Defense in depth assumes any single layer, including the perimeter, could fail and adds additional protective layers, while perimeter-only security depends heavily on the outer boundary holding", "Defense in depth uses fewer controls than perimeter-only security", "Perimeter security includes more layers than defense in depth", "They are identical strategies"], "answerIndex": 0, "explanation": "Defense in depth adds multiple layers beyond the perimeter, unlike a strategy that depends mainly on the outer boundary."},
        {"topic": "microsoft_defender_for_cloud", "question": "What is the primary purpose of Microsoft Defender for Cloud?", "choices": ["To provide security posture management and threat protection for cloud and hybrid workloads", "To manage billing and cost allocation across subscriptions", "To provide file synchronization between on-prem servers and Azure Files", "To assign RBAC roles automatically"], "answerIndex": 0, "explanation": "Defender for Cloud focuses on assessing security posture and protecting workloads across cloud and hybrid environments."},
