        {"topic": "factors_affecting_costs", "question": "An organization is deciding between pay-as-you-go pricing and reserved capacity for a workload that will run continuously for three years. Which factor should most influence this decision?", "choices": ["The color scheme of the Azure portal", "Predictable long-term usage that could benefit from reserved capacity discounts", "The number of tags applied to the resource", "The subscription's display name"], "answerIndex": 1, "explanation": "Reserved capacity offers discounts in exchange for a committed usage term, making it well suited to predictable, long-running workloads."},
        {"topic": "factors_affecting_costs", "question": "Which of these is a factor that can affect the cost of an Azure resource?", "choices": ["The resource's display icon", "Resource type and configured usage or consumption meters", "The user's web browser", "The time zone of the administrator's laptop"], "answerIndex": 1, "explanation": "Cost is driven by what type of resource is deployed and how much of its billable usage meters (like hours or GB) are consumed."},
        {"topic": "factors_affecting_costs", "question": "Compared to a single-region deployment, a multi-region deployment with data replication will typically:", "choices": ["Always cost less due to automatic bulk discounts", "Cost exactly the same because region has no effect on billing", "Potentially cost more due to additional resource instances and inter-region data transfer", "Never be billed at all"], "answerIndex": 2, "explanation": "Running duplicate resources in multiple regions and transferring data between them adds usage and data-transfer charges beyond a single-region setup."},
        {"topic": "pricing_calculator", "question": "What is the primary purpose of the Azure Pricing Calculator?", "choices": ["To monitor real-time spend on an active subscription", "To estimate the cost of Azure services before deployment", "To apply resource locks to resources", "To create Azure Policy definitions"], "answerIndex": 1, "explanation": "The Pricing Calculator is designed to help estimate costs in advance, before any resources are actually deployed."},
        {"topic": "pricing_calculator", "question": "A prospective customer wants to know roughly how much a planned set of virtual machines and storage accounts will cost per month before committing to Azure. Which tool should they use?", "choices": ["Azure Monitor", "Azure Pricing Calculator", "Azure Advisor", "Azure Policy"], "answerIndex": 1, "explanation": "The Pricing Calculator is built specifically for estimating costs of planned services prior to deployment."},
        {"topic": "pricing_calculator", "question": "How does the Pricing Calculator differ from Cost Management + Billing?", "choices": ["They are identical tools with different names", "The Pricing Calculator estimates future costs before deployment, while Cost Management analyzes actual incurred spend", "Cost Management is only for on-premises resources", "The Pricing Calculator tracks real invoices after billing"], "answerIndex": 1, "explanation": "The Pricing Calculator is a pre-deployment estimation tool, whereas Cost Management analyzes real, already-incurred spending."},
        {"topic": "pricing_calculator", "question": "Which statement about the Azure Pricing Calculator is correct?", "choices": ["It requires an active paid Azure subscription to use", "It can be used without signing in to produce a cost estimate", "It only estimates costs for virtual machines", "It automatically deploys the resources you configure"], "answerIndex": 1, "explanation": "The Pricing Calculator is publicly accessible and can generate estimates without requiring an existing Azure subscription."},
        {"topic": "pricing_calculator", "question": "In the Pricing Calculator, what can a user typically adjust to refine a cost estimate?", "choices": ["Only the currency symbol displayed", "Region, tier, and quantity or usage of a service", "The Azure AD tenant name", "The support plan of another customer"], "answerIndex": 1, "explanation": "The calculator lets users configure parameters like region, service tier, and expected usage to produce a tailored estimate."},
        {"topic": "pricing_calculator", "question": "A student creates an estimate for a virtual machine in the Pricing Calculator but doesn't purchase anything. What happens?", "choices": ["The VM is automatically deployed", "They are billed immediately for the estimate", "No resources are created or billed, since it is only an estimate", "Azure locks their account for testing pricing"], "answerIndex": 2, "explanation": "The Pricing Calculator only produces an estimate and does not provision or bill for any actual resources."},
        {"topic": "pricing_calculator", "question": "Which best describes the relationship between the Pricing Calculator and the Total Cost of Ownership (TCO) Calculator?", "choices": ["They are the same tool under different names", "The Pricing Calculator estimates Azure service costs, while the TCO Calculator compares on-premises costs to estimated Azure costs", "The TCO Calculator only estimates storage costs", "The Pricing Calculator replaces the need for any billing statements"], "answerIndex": 1, "explanation": "The Pricing Calculator focuses on estimating Azure service costs, while the TCO Calculator is designed to compare existing on-premises costs against projected Azure costs."},
        {"topic": "cost_management_capabilities", "question": "What is a primary capability of Azure Cost Management?", "choices": ["Deploying virtual networks", "Analyzing and monitoring Azure spending", "Managing Active Directory user accounts", "Configuring DNS zones"], "answerIndex": 1, "explanation": "Cost Management is focused on giving visibility into and control over Azure spending, not resource deployment or identity."},
        {"topic": "cost_management_capabilities", "question": "A finance team wants to be notified automatically when monthly Azure spending exceeds a set dollar amount. What should they configure?", "choices": ["A resource lock", "A budget with an alert in Cost Management", "An Azure Policy assignment", "An Azure Service Health alert"], "answerIndex": 1, "explanation": "Budgets in Cost Management let organizations define spending thresholds and receive alerts when they are approached or exceeded."},
        {"topic": "cost_management_capabilities", "question": "How do budgets differ from cost analysis in Azure Cost Management?", "choices": ["Budgets only show historical data while cost analysis sets spending limits", "Budgets set spending thresholds and alerts, while cost analysis visualizes and breaks down actual spending", "They are exactly the same feature with different names", "Budgets require Azure Policy to function at all"], "answerIndex": 1, "explanation": "Budgets are about setting and alerting on spending limits, while cost analysis is about visualizing and exploring where money has actually been spent."},
        {"topic": "cost_management_capabilities", "question": "Which statement about Azure Cost Management is accurate?", "choices": ["It can only display costs for the current day", "It can break down costs by resource group, service, or tag", "It automatically deletes overspending resources", "It is only available to Enterprise Agreement customers"], "answerIndex": 1, "explanation": "Cost Management provides flexible views that let users break down and filter spending by dimensions such as resource group, service, and tag."},
        {"topic": "cost_management_capabilities", "question": "Which of the following can Azure Cost Management help an organization do?", "choices": ["Design network topology", "Identify cost trends and underutilized resources", "Manage user passwords", "Configure firewall rules"], "answerIndex": 1, "explanation": "Cost Management's core function is analyzing spending trends and helping identify opportunities to reduce waste and cost."},
        {"topic": "cost_management_capabilities", "question": "An administrator notices unexpected spend in a subscription and wants to see which resource group is responsible. Which capability provides this breakdown?", "choices": ["Azure Advisor recommendations only", "Azure Cost Management cost analysis", "Azure Arc", "Resource locks"], "answerIndex": 1, "explanation": "Cost analysis within Cost Management allows spend to be filtered and grouped by resource group, service, and other dimensions."},
        {"topic": "cost_management_capabilities", "question": "Compared to simply reviewing a monthly invoice, what advantage does Cost Management's budget alerting provide?", "choices": ["None, invoices already provide proactive alerts", "It can proactively notify stakeholders before or when spending thresholds are crossed, rather than after the fact", "Invoices are always more detailed than any Cost Management feature", "Alerts replace the need for billing altogether"], "answerIndex": 1, "explanation": "Budget alerts notify stakeholders in near real time as spending approaches limits, rather than waiting for a completed invoice."},
        {"topic": "purpose_of_tags", "question": "What are Azure tags primarily used for?", "choices": ["Encrypting resource data", "Organizing and categorizing resources with metadata", "Creating virtual networks", "Granting role-based access permissions"], "answerIndex": 1, "explanation": "Tags are metadata labels used to organize and categorize resources, not a security, networking, or encryption mechanism."},
        {"topic": "purpose_of_tags", "question": "A company wants to see the total monthly cost incurred by its Marketing department across resources spread over multiple resource groups. What should they use?", "choices": ["Resource locks", "Consistent tags applied to relevant resources, then filter cost reports by tag", "Azure Policy deny rules alone", "Azure Arc"], "answerIndex": 1, "explanation": "Tags can be applied across resource groups and used to filter or aggregate cost and reporting data by category, such as department."},
        {"topic": "purpose_of_tags", "question": "How do tags differ from resource groups as an organizational mechanism?", "choices": ["They are functionally identical", "Resource groups are a required container for deployment, while tags are optional metadata that can cross resource group boundaries", "Tags can only be applied to virtual machines", "Resource groups cannot contain any tagged resources"], "answerIndex": 1, "explanation": "Every resource must live in a resource group, but tags are optional labels that can be applied consistently across resources in different resource groups."},
        {"topic": "purpose_of_tags", "question": "Which statement about Azure tags is correct?", "choices": ["A resource can only have one tag at a time", "Tags are simple name and value pairs, and a resource can have multiple tags", "Tags automatically enforce security policies", "Tags cannot be used for cost reporting"], "answerIndex": 1, "explanation": "Tags consist of a name and a value, and multiple tags can be assigned to the same resource for flexible categorization."},
        {"topic": "purpose_of_tags", "question": "What format do Azure tags use?", "choices": ["XML schema", "Name and value pairs", "Binary flags", "SQL queries"], "answerIndex": 1, "explanation": "Tags are simple key (name) and value pairs attached to resources as metadata."},
        {"topic": "purpose_of_tags", "question": "An administrator wants to identify all resources belonging to a specific project regardless of which resource group they are in. What is the most efficient approach?", "choices": ["Manually inspect every resource group", "Apply a consistent Project tag to relevant resources and filter by that tag", "Rename every resource individually", "Create a brand-new subscription for the project"], "answerIndex": 1, "explanation": "A consistently applied tag lets resources be identified and filtered across resource group boundaries without manual inspection."},
        {"topic": "purpose_of_tags", "question": "In terms of governance, how do tags complement Azure Policy?", "choices": ["They are unrelated features", "Azure Policy can require or enforce specific tags on resources, combining organizational metadata with governance rules", "Tags fully replace the need for Azure Policy", "Azure Policy automatically removes all tags"], "answerIndex": 1, "explanation": "Azure Policy can be used to mandate that resources carry certain tags, tying tagging conventions to enforced governance."},
        {"topic": "microsoft_purview", "question": "What is the primary purpose of Microsoft Purview?", "choices": ["Deploying virtual machines", "Data governance, cataloging, and compliance management", "Managing network routing", "Billing and invoicing"], "answerIndex": 1, "explanation": "Purview is designed to help organizations discover, catalog, classify, and govern their data for compliance purposes."},
        {"topic": "microsoft_purview", "question": "An organization needs to discover and classify sensitive data scattered across on-premises databases, Azure storage, and SaaS applications. Which service is designed for this?", "choices": ["Azure Advisor", "Microsoft Purview", "Azure Arc", "Resource locks"], "answerIndex": 1, "explanation": "Purview specializes in discovering and classifying data across hybrid, multi-cloud, and SaaS environments."},
        {"topic": "microsoft_purview", "question": "How does Microsoft Purview relate to Azure Policy?", "choices": ["They perform exactly the same function", "Purview focuses on data governance and compliance such as cataloging and classifying data, while Azure Policy enforces rules about resource configuration", "Azure Policy manages data catalogs directly", "Purview replaces Azure Policy entirely"], "answerIndex": 1, "explanation": "Purview governs data itself (discovery, classification, protection), while Azure Policy governs the configuration and compliance of Azure resources."},
        {"topic": "microsoft_purview", "question": "Which statement about Microsoft Purview is accurate?", "choices": ["It can only catalog data stored within Azure", "It provides governance capabilities across on-premises, multi-cloud, and SaaS data sources", "It is exclusively a networking tool", "It cannot classify sensitive data"], "answerIndex": 1, "explanation": "Purview is designed to extend data governance beyond Azure alone, covering on-premises, multi-cloud, and SaaS data sources."},
        {"topic": "microsoft_purview", "question": "Which capability is offered by Microsoft Purview?", "choices": ["Virtual network peering", "Data catalog and data classification", "Load balancing traffic", "DNS resolution"], "answerIndex": 1, "explanation": "Cataloging and classifying data is a core capability of Purview, unlike the other listed networking-focused options."},
        {"topic": "microsoft_purview", "question": "A compliance officer wants to understand where personally identifiable information resides across the company's data estate to satisfy regulatory requirements. Which tool helps most directly?", "choices": ["Azure Monitor", "Microsoft Purview", "Azure Cloud Shell", "ARM templates"], "answerIndex": 1, "explanation": "Purview is purpose-built to map and classify sensitive data like PII across an organization's entire data estate."},
        {"topic": "microsoft_purview", "question": "Compared to a basic storage account access control list, how does Purview add value for governance?", "choices": ["It provides no additional value", "It adds data-level discovery, classification, and compliance insight across the broader data estate, beyond simple access control", "It replaces the need for any access control", "It only works with a single storage account"], "answerIndex": 1, "explanation": "Purview goes beyond access control by discovering, cataloging, and classifying data across many sources, giving a broader compliance picture."},
        {"topic": "azure_policy", "question": "What is the main purpose of Azure Policy?", "choices": ["Estimating costs before deployment", "Enforcing organizational rules and compliance on resources", "Providing a command-line interface", "Cataloging sensitive data"], "answerIndex": 1, "explanation": "Azure Policy exists to define and enforce rules that keep resources compliant with organizational or regulatory standards."},
        {"topic": "azure_policy", "question": "An organization wants to prevent anyone from creating storage accounts that allow public blob access. Which service should they configure?", "choices": ["Azure Advisor", "Azure Policy with a deny effect", "Azure Pricing Calculator", "Resource tags alone"], "answerIndex": 1, "explanation": "A deny-effect Azure Policy can block the creation of resources that violate a defined configuration rule, such as allowing public access."},
        {"topic": "azure_policy", "question": "How does Azure Policy differ from Azure RBAC (role-based access control)?", "choices": ["They are identical in function", "RBAC controls what actions users can perform, while Azure Policy controls the properties and configuration of resources regardless of who created them", "Azure Policy manages user identities", "RBAC enforces resource tagging rules"], "answerIndex": 1, "explanation": "RBAC governs user permissions, while Azure Policy governs resource configuration and compliance independent of the acting user's role."},
        {"topic": "azure_policy", "question": "Which statement about Azure Policy is correct?", "choices": ["Azure Policy can only report on compliance and cannot prevent resource creation", "Azure Policy can audit, deny, or remediate resources based on the effect defined in the policy", "Policies cannot be grouped together", "Azure Policy only applies to virtual machines"], "answerIndex": 1, "explanation": "Depending on the configured effect, Azure Policy can audit, deny, or even remediate non-compliant resources, and it applies across many resource types."},
        {"topic": "azure_policy", "question": "What is an Azure Policy initiative?", "choices": ["A single user account", "A collection of related policies grouped together for a broader compliance goal", "A billing report", "A type of resource lock"], "answerIndex": 1, "explanation": "An initiative bundles multiple related policies so they can be assigned and tracked together toward a larger compliance objective."},
        {"topic": "azure_policy", "question": "A company must ensure all resources deployed in a subscription are tagged with a CostCenter value. Which service can enforce this requirement automatically?", "choices": ["Azure Monitor", "Azure Policy", "Azure Cloud Shell", "Microsoft Purview"], "answerIndex": 1, "explanation": "Azure Policy can require specific tags on resources and deny or flag deployments that don't comply."},
        {"topic": "azure_policy", "question": "Compared to a resource lock, what makes Azure Policy more suited for enforcing organization-wide configuration standards?", "choices": ["Resource locks can define complex conditional rules across many resources while Policy cannot", "Azure Policy can evaluate and enforce configuration rules at scale based on conditions, while resource locks simply prevent deletion or modification of specific individual resources", "They perform an identical function", "Resource locks are only used for cost control"], "answerIndex": 1, "explanation": "Azure Policy applies condition-based rules broadly across resources, while a resource lock only protects a specific resource from deletion or change."},
        {"topic": "resource_locks", "question": "What is the primary purpose of an Azure resource lock?", "choices": ["To reduce billing costs", "To prevent accidental deletion or modification of resources", "To classify sensitive data", "To provide command-line access"], "answerIndex": 1, "explanation": "Resource locks exist specifically to guard against accidental deletion or modification of important resources."},
        {"topic": "resource_locks", "question": "An administrator wants to ensure a production virtual network can never be accidentally deleted, even by users with Owner permissions. What should they apply?", "choices": ["A CanNotDelete resource lock", "A tag", "A budget alert", "An Azure Policy audit rule"], "answerIndex": 0, "explanation": "A CanNotDelete lock blocks deletion of the resource for all users, including those with Owner role permissions."},
        {"topic": "resource_locks", "question": "What is the difference between the CanNotDelete and ReadOnly lock types?", "choices": ["They are identical", "CanNotDelete blocks deletion but allows modification, while ReadOnly blocks both modification and deletion", "ReadOnly only blocks deletion, not modification", "CanNotDelete blocks all read access to the resource"], "answerIndex": 1, "explanation": "CanNotDelete still permits changes to a resource, whereas ReadOnly locks down both modification and deletion."},
        {"topic": "resource_locks", "question": "Which statement about resource locks is accurate?", "choices": ["Locks can be bypassed by users with the Owner role", "Locks apply regardless of a user's assigned role-based access permissions", "Locks only apply to storage accounts", "Locks automatically expire after 30 days"], "answerIndex": 1, "explanation": "Resource locks override RBAC permissions, meaning even Owners cannot delete or modify a locked resource without first removing the lock."},
        {"topic": "resource_locks", "question": "At which scopes can an Azure resource lock be applied?", "choices": ["Only individual resources", "Subscription, resource group, or individual resource", "Only management groups", "Only Azure AD tenants"], "answerIndex": 1, "explanation": "Locks can be applied at the subscription, resource group, or individual resource level, and are inherited by child resources."},
        {"topic": "resource_locks", "question": "A team applies a ReadOnly lock to a resource group containing a storage account. What effect does this have?", "choices": ["Users can still delete the storage account", "Users cannot modify or delete resources in the group, and some read operations like retrieving storage keys may also be affected", "The lock only affects resources added after the lock is applied", "It has no effect on existing resources"], "answerIndex": 1, "explanation": "A ReadOnly lock prevents both modification and deletion, and because it is inherited by child resources, some operations that behave like writes (such as listing keys) can also be blocked."},
        {"topic": "resource_locks", "question": "How do resource locks differ from Azure Policy deny rules in terms of what they protect against?", "choices": ["They protect against the same thing in the same way", "Resource locks prevent modification or deletion of existing specific resources, while Azure Policy deny rules primarily prevent non-compliant resources from being created in the first place", "Resource locks are used only during resource creation", "Azure Policy only works after a resource has been deleted"], "answerIndex": 1, "explanation": "Locks protect existing resources from unwanted changes, while Policy deny rules stop non-compliant resources from being created in the first place."},
        {"topic": "azure_portal", "question": "What is the Azure portal?", "choices": ["A command-line-only interface", "A web-based graphical interface for managing Azure resources", "A desktop application requiring installation", "A billing invoice document"], "answerIndex": 1, "explanation": "The Azure portal is a browser-based graphical interface used to build and manage Azure resources."},
        {"topic": "azure_portal", "question": "A new administrator prefers a visual, point-and-click way to create and manage resources rather than writing scripts. Which tool should they use?", "choices": ["Azure CLI", "Azure PowerShell", "Azure portal", "Raw ARM template JSON files"], "answerIndex": 2, "explanation": "The Azure portal offers a graphical, wizard-driven experience suited to users who prefer clicking through options over scripting."},
        {"topic": "azure_portal", "question": "How does the Azure portal differ from Azure CLI in terms of interaction style?", "choices": ["They are the same tool", "The portal offers a graphical, browser-based interface, while the CLI offers a command-line, scriptable interface", "The CLI is only used for billing tasks", "The portal cannot be used to manage any resources"], "answerIndex": 1, "explanation": "The portal is visual and browser-based, while the CLI is a text-based, scriptable command interface."},
        {"topic": "azure_portal", "question": "Which statement about the Azure portal is correct?", "choices": ["It requires a separate desktop installation on each machine", "It is accessible via a web browser and can be customized with dashboards and favorites", "It has no accessibility features", "It cannot be used to access Cloud Shell"], "answerIndex": 1, "explanation": "The portal runs in a browser and supports personalization like custom dashboards and favorites, along with accessibility features."},
        {"topic": "azure_portal", "question": "Which of the following can be customized in the Azure portal?", "choices": ["The underlying Azure datacenter hardware", "Dashboards and favorite resources for a personalized view", "The Azure global network backbone", "Other users' passwords"], "answerIndex": 1, "explanation": "Users can personalize their portal experience with custom dashboards and pinned favorite resources."},
        {"topic": "azure_portal", "question": "A visually impaired administrator needs to manage Azure resources with screen reader support. What supports this need?", "choices": ["Azure CLI only", "The Azure portal's built-in accessibility features", "ARM templates", "Resource locks"], "answerIndex": 1, "explanation": "The Azure portal includes accessibility features such as screen reader compatibility and high-contrast themes."},
        {"topic": "azure_portal", "question": "Compared to using ARM templates directly, what advantage does the Azure portal provide for a first-time user creating a single VM?", "choices": ["None, they are equally complex", "The portal provides a guided, visual wizard-style experience that doesn't require writing JSON syntax", "ARM templates are simpler for beginners", "The portal cannot create virtual machines"], "answerIndex": 1, "explanation": "The portal walks users through resource creation visually, which is generally more approachable for beginners than writing JSON templates."},
        {"topic": "cloud_shell_cli_powershell", "question": "What is Azure Cloud Shell?", "choices": ["A desktop-only application", "A browser-accessible shell environment for managing Azure without local installation", "A billing dashboard", "A physical datacenter management tool"], "answerIndex": 1, "explanation": "Cloud Shell runs in the browser and requires no local software installation to manage Azure resources."},
        {"topic": "cloud_shell_cli_powershell", "question": "An administrator is working from a locked-down machine where they cannot install any software but needs command-line access to Azure. What should they use?", "choices": ["Install Azure CLI locally", "Azure Cloud Shell via the browser", "The ARM template designer only", "Resource locks"], "answerIndex": 1, "explanation": "Cloud Shell runs entirely in the browser, so it works even when local software installation isn't possible."},
        {"topic": "cloud_shell_cli_powershell", "question": "How does Azure CLI differ from Azure PowerShell?", "choices": ["They are functionally identical with no syntax differences", "Azure CLI uses command syntax common across platforms with commands starting with 'az', while Azure PowerShell uses PowerShell cmdlet syntax familiar to Windows administrators", "Azure PowerShell only runs inside Cloud Shell", "Azure CLI cannot be used in scripts"], "answerIndex": 1, "explanation": "Azure CLI and Azure PowerShell offer equivalent capabilities but use different command syntaxes suited to different scripting backgrounds."},
        {"topic": "cloud_shell_cli_powershell", "question": "Which statement about Azure Cloud Shell is accurate?", "choices": ["It requires downloading a separate application to your desktop", "It supports both Bash and PowerShell environments directly in the browser", "It can only be used to view billing information", "It is unavailable from the Azure portal"], "answerIndex": 1, "explanation": "Cloud Shell offers a choice between Bash and PowerShell, and it runs directly in the browser, including from within the Azure portal."},
        {"topic": "cloud_shell_cli_powershell", "question": "What is a defining characteristic of Azure CLI commands?", "choices": ["They are written in XML", "They are typically invoked with the 'az' prefix followed by a command and parameters", "They require a graphical interface to run", "They can only run on Linux"], "answerIndex": 1, "explanation": "Azure CLI commands follow a consistent pattern starting with 'az', making them concise and scriptable across platforms."},
        {"topic": "cloud_shell_cli_powershell", "question": "A Windows systems administrator with years of PowerShell scripting experience wants to automate Azure resource deployment using familiar syntax and cmdlets. Which tool best fits this need?", "choices": ["Azure CLI exclusively", "Azure PowerShell", "The Azure portal only", "Azure Advisor"], "answerIndex": 1, "explanation": "Azure PowerShell provides cmdlets that align with existing PowerShell scripting knowledge, making automation more natural for this administrator."},
        {"topic": "cloud_shell_cli_powershell", "question": "Compared to the Azure portal, what advantage do Azure CLI and Azure PowerShell provide?", "choices": ["They provide no advantages over the portal", "They enable scripting and automation of repeatable tasks, which is harder to achieve through manual portal clicks", "They cannot manage any Azure resources", "They are only available to Microsoft employees"], "answerIndex": 1, "explanation": "Command-line tools allow tasks to be scripted and repeated reliably, which is more efficient than manually repeating steps in the portal."},
        {"topic": "azure_arc", "question": "What is the primary purpose of Azure Arc?", "choices": ["To provide a pricing calculator", "To extend Azure management to resources outside Azure, including on-premises and other clouds", "To lock resources against deletion", "To catalog sensitive data"], "answerIndex": 1, "explanation": "Azure Arc's core function is bringing non-Azure resources under Azure's management and governance umbrella."},
        {"topic": "azure_arc", "question": "A company runs servers in its own datacenter and in another cloud provider but wants to apply consistent Azure Policy governance and RBAC across all of them from one place. What should they use?", "choices": ["Azure Advisor", "Azure Arc", "Azure Pricing Calculator", "Resource locks"], "answerIndex": 1, "explanation": "Azure Arc projects non-Azure resources into Azure Resource Manager so they can be governed with tools like Azure Policy and RBAC."},
        {"topic": "azure_arc", "question": "How does Azure Arc differ from a typical Azure resource deployed natively in Azure?", "choices": ["There is no difference", "Arc-enabled resources physically run outside Azure but are represented and managed within Azure Resource Manager, unlike native resources that run within Azure's own datacenters", "Arc resources cannot be tagged", "Native Azure resources cannot use Azure Policy"], "answerIndex": 1, "explanation": "Arc-enabled resources remain physically outside Azure but appear as manageable resources within Azure Resource Manager."},
        {"topic": "azure_arc", "question": "Which statement about Azure Arc is correct?", "choices": ["It only supports managing virtual machines", "It can extend management to servers, Kubernetes clusters, and databases outside Azure", "It requires all resources to be physically migrated into Azure", "It cannot use Azure Policy on connected resources"], "answerIndex": 1, "explanation": "Azure Arc supports multiple resource types beyond servers, including Kubernetes clusters and databases, without requiring migration."},
        {"topic": "azure_arc", "question": "What does Azure Arc allow organizations to do with non-Azure resources?", "choices": ["Physically relocate them into an Azure datacenter", "Manage and govern them using Azure tools as if they were native Azure resources", "Delete them automatically", "Convert them into ARM templates without any registration step"], "answerIndex": 1, "explanation": "Arc lets organizations apply familiar Azure management tools to resources that remain outside Azure physically."},
        {"topic": "azure_arc", "question": "A hybrid organization wants a single view to check compliance status across on-premises Windows servers and Azure virtual machines alike. Which combination enables this?", "choices": ["Azure Arc combined with Azure Policy", "Azure Pricing Calculator alone", "Resource locks alone", "Tags alone"], "answerIndex": 0, "explanation": "Connecting on-premises servers via Azure Arc lets Azure Policy evaluate and report their compliance alongside native Azure resources."},
        {"topic": "azure_arc", "question": "Compared to managing on-premises servers with separate, disconnected tools, what benefit does Azure Arc provide?", "choices": ["No benefit; it duplicates existing tools", "It provides a consistent, centralized management and governance experience alongside native Azure resources", "It removes the need for any on-premises infrastructure", "It only works with Linux servers"], "answerIndex": 1, "explanation": "Azure Arc unifies management of hybrid and multi-cloud resources under a single, consistent set of Azure tools."},
        {"topic": "infrastructure_as_code", "question": "What best defines Infrastructure as Code?", "choices": ["Manually clicking through a portal to create resources", "Managing and provisioning infrastructure through machine-readable definition files", "A billing report format", "A type of resource lock"], "answerIndex": 1, "explanation": "IaC is defined by using code-like definition files to manage infrastructure, rather than manual, one-off configuration."},
        {"topic": "infrastructure_as_code", "question": "A team wants every environment (dev, test, production) to be deployed with identical, repeatable configurations and wants to track changes over time using version control. What approach should they adopt?", "choices": ["Manually configuring each environment separately", "Infrastructure as Code", "Applying resource locks only", "Reviewing invoices monthly"], "answerIndex": 1, "explanation": "IaC enables version-controlled, repeatable deployment definitions that can be reused consistently across environments."},
        {"topic": "infrastructure_as_code", "question": "How does IaC-based deployment differ from manually provisioning resources through the Azure portal?", "choices": ["They produce identical guarantees of consistency", "IaC uses version-controlled, repeatable definition files, reducing the risk of manual configuration drift, while manual portal changes are harder to track and reproduce consistently", "The portal itself is a form of IaC", "IaC cannot be automated"], "answerIndex": 1, "explanation": "IaC's defining strength is repeatable, trackable deployments, which manual portal-based configuration doesn't guarantee."},
